WEBVTT

00:57:29.500 --> 00:57:31.976
This is, I find I don't --

00:57:32.000 --> 00:57:34.226
you're the one who studies
these things, right?

00:57:34.250 --> 00:57:35.976
I don't know how to interpret that.

00:57:36.000 --> 00:57:37.268
(Laughter)

00:57:37.292 --> 00:57:40.934
AG: The only thing that jumped to mind
on this is I think Oliver Burkeman

00:57:40.958 --> 00:57:42.851
covered the best antidote I've seen.

00:57:42.875 --> 00:57:45.018
He wrote this great
little article that said,

00:57:45.042 --> 00:57:48.684
"Look, every time you feel FOMO,
you should erase it,

00:57:48.708 --> 00:57:52.559
and instead turn it into JOMO,
the joy of missing out."

00:57:52.583 --> 00:57:55.059
And I thought this was
such a clever concept to say,

00:57:55.083 --> 00:57:58.601
anytime you're afraid you're going to
miss out on a great job opportunity

00:57:58.625 --> 00:58:00.226
or even a social event, right?